-- title:
--   A semistable witness package for J₀(Nq) at q∤ N
-- statement:
--   Setting. Let $N\ge 1$, let $q$ be a prime with $q\nmid N$ (hypotheses `hq`, `hqN`), and let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $q$ in the sense of `hA : A.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. the image of $q$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ is a non-unit of $A$; consequently the residue field $\kappa :=$ `ResidueField A` has characteristic $q$. Write $F :=$ `modularFunctionFieldC` $\kappa\,N$ for the subfield of the Laurent series field over $\kappa$ generated by `jqModC` $\kappa$ and `jqNModC` $\kappa\,N$, and $g :=$ `arithFrobC q` $\kappa\,N$ for the semilinear automorphism of $F$ over $\kappa$ obtained from the $q$-power Frobenius of $\kappa$ acting on Laurent coefficients (an element of the group `SemilinearAut` $\kappa\,F$ of compatible pairs of ring automorphisms). Write $H :=$ `inertiaInvariants A (N * q)`, the subgroup of $J_0(Nq) :=$ `JZero (N * q)` $= \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the level-$Nq$ modular function field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ consisting of the elements fixed by the image `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` of the inertia subgroup of $A$ in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$. Both `JZero (N * q)` and `JZero N` carry the `HeckeAlg`-module structure `heckeModuleBar`, where `HeckeAlg` $= \mathbb Z[X_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$ with generators `heckeGen ℓ`.
--
--   Hypotheses on the node data. For every finite set $W$ of places of $F$ over $\kappa$ subject to two hypotheses: `hW`, which says that a place $w$ belongs to $W$ if and only if $w \in$ `ssPlaces q N` $\kappa$, i.e. `IsSupersingularPlace q N` $\kappa\,w$ holds; and `hstab`, which says that the finite set of node pairs $S :=$ `nodePairsOfPlaces` $g\,W$ — the image of $W$ under the embedding `smulNodePairEmb` $g$ — is node-stable for $g$, that is, $(g\cdot s_1, g\cdot s_2)\in S$ for every $s\in S$.
--
--   Assertion. There exist: a modular polynomial datum `data : ModularPolynomialData q` (a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb Z[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ annihilating the $q$-th modular $q$-expansion) together with `hKr : KroneckerCongruence q data`, the Kronecker congruence that the bivariate reduction of $\Phi$ modulo $q$ equals $(C(X)^q - X)(C(X) - X^q)$; the integrality statements `hα : HeckeAlphaBarIntegral (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) N q` and `hβ : HeckeBetaBarIntegral (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) N q`, asserting that the ring homomorphisms underlying `heckeAlphaBar` and `heckeBetaBar` at level $N$ and prime $q$ are integral; a place specialisation $P :$ `PlaceSpecialization A q N data hKr` $\kappa$ `(IsLocalRing.residue A)` $h\alpha\,h\beta$, consisting of a map `P.sp` from places of the level-$N$ modular function field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ to places of $F$, a homomorphism `P.spPic0 : JZero N →+ Pic0` $\kappa\,F$, and the order-compatibility data of that structure relative to the residue map of $A$; a prolongation tuple $R :$ `P.ProlongationTuple`, consisting of a lift `redBar` of the residue map to $\kappa$, an embedding $\iota$ of the full level-$N$ modular function field over $\kappa$ into $F$ compatible with coefficientwise reduction, two regular prolongations $R_1, R_2$ of $A$ to the level-$Nq$ modular function field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with values in the full level-$N$ function field over $\kappa$, and the compatibilities of that structure (including the one linking $R_2$ with $R_1$ through the Atkin–Lehner involution `ProlongationTuple.atkinLehnerBar`); a width function $e :$ Place $\kappa\,F \to \mathbb N$; an additive map $\mathrm{comp} : H \to$ `componentGroup (widthOfPlaces` $g\,W\,e)$, the cokernel of the Gram map attached to the widths $s \mapsto e(s_1)$ on $S$ inside the $\mathbb Z$-dual of the character lattice; and an additive map $\mathrm{sp} : H \to$ `GluedPic0` $\kappa\,F\,S$, the group of admissible gluing data (a pair of degree-zero divisors vanishing at the relevant components of the node pairs, together with a family of units of $\kappa$ indexed by $S$) modulo glued principal data; such that all of the following hold.
--
--   (1) $\mathrm{comp}$ is surjective.
--
--   (2) For $x \in H$, $\mathrm{comp}\,x = 0$ if and only if `P.IsGoodClass` $S$ holds for the class $x \in J_0(Nq)$, i.e. $x$ is represented by a degree-zero divisor $D$ satisfying the predicate `P.IsGoodDiv` for which the gluing datum `P.glueData` $S\,D$ is admissible.
--
--   (3) `P.IsGluedSpecialization` $S\,\mathrm{sp}$: for every degree-zero divisor $D$ at level $Nq$ whose class lies in $H$ and every admissible gluing datum $x$, if $D$ satisfies `P.IsGoodDiv` and $x$ equals `P.glueData` $S\,D$, then $\mathrm{sp}$ of the class of $D$ is the class of $x$ in `GluedPic0` $\kappa\,F\,S$.
--
--   (4) There exist a `HeckeAlg`-module structure on $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F)$ and an additive map $\mathrm{sp}_N :$ `JZero N` $\to \mathrm{Pic}^0(F)$ for which the following twenty-four clauses hold, where `toPic0Pair` $S$ denotes the forgetful map from `GluedPic0` $\kappa\,F\,S$ to $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F)\times \mathrm{Pic}^0(F)$.
--
--   (4a) $e\,w > 0$ for every $w \in W$.
--
--   (4b) $\mathrm{comp}$ is surjective (repeated).
--
--   (4c) For every prime $\ell \nmid Nq$ and every $x \in H$ with `heckeGen ℓ` $\cdot x \in H$, one has $\mathrm{comp}(T_\ell x) = (\ell+1)\,\mathrm{comp}(x)$.
--
--   (4d) For every $T \in$ `HeckeAlg` and $x \in H$ with $T\cdot x \in H$: $\mathrm{comp}\,x = 0$ implies $\mathrm{comp}(T\cdot x) = 0$.
--
--   (4e) For every $\varphi \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ with `A.IsFrobeniusAt` $\varphi\,q$ (that is, $\varphi$ lies in the decomposition subgroup of $A$ and acts on $\kappa$ by $x\mapsto x^q$) and every $x \in H$ with $\varphi\cdot x \in H$: $\mathrm{comp}\,x = 0$ implies $\mathrm{comp}(\varphi\cdot x) = 0$.
--
--   (4f) For every prime $\ell \nmid Nq$ and every $x \in H$ with $T_\ell x \in H$ and $\mathrm{comp}\,x = 0$: if `HeckeInputsFibre` $\kappa\,N\,\ell$ holds (existence of principal divisors on the degeneracy roof, integrality of the two fibre correspondences, and descent of `heckeDivFibre` to $\mathrm{Pic}^0$), then `toPic0Pair` $S(\mathrm{sp}(T_\ell x))$ is obtained from `toPic0Pair` $S(\mathrm{sp}\,x)$ by applying `heckePic0Fibre` $\kappa\,N\,\ell$ to each of its two components.
--
--   (4g) For every $T \in$ `HeckeAlg` and $x \in H$ with $T\cdot x \in H$: if $\mathrm{comp}\,x = 0$ and `toPic0Pair` $S(\mathrm{sp}\,x) = 0$, then `toPic0Pair` $S(\mathrm{sp}(T\cdot x)) = 0$.
--
--   (4h) For every $\varphi$ with `A.IsFrobeniusAt` $\varphi\,q$ and every $x \in H$ with $\varphi\cdot x \in H$ and $\mathrm{comp}\,x = 0$: $\mathrm{sp}(\varphi\cdot x) =$ `GluedPic0.glueMap` $S\,g\,$`hstab` $(\mathrm{sp}\,x)$, the endomorphism of the glued Picard group induced by the arithmetic Frobenius.
--
--   (4i) For every $x \in H$ with $T_q x \in H$ and $\mathrm{comp}\,x = 0$: for every family $u : S \to \mathrm{Additive}(\kappa^\times)$, if $\mathrm{sp}\,x =$ `GluedPic0.nodeUnit` $S\,u$ (the class of the gluing datum with zero divisors and node units $u$), then $\mathrm{sp}(T_q x) =$ `GluedPic0.nodeUnit` $S$ of $u$ precomposed with the inverse of the node permutation `SemilinearAut.nodePerm` $S\,g\,$`hstab`.
--
--   (4j) For $x \in H$: if $x$ is prime-to-$q$ torsion (`PrimeToTorsion q`: some $n>0$ with $q\nmid n$ kills $x$), $\mathrm{comp}\,x = 0$ and $\mathrm{sp}\,x = 0$, then $x = 0$.
--
--   (4k) For every prime $\ell \nmid Nq$ and every $x \in H$ with $T_\ell x \in H$ and $\mathrm{comp}\,x = 0$: `toPic0Pair` $S(\mathrm{sp}(T_\ell x)) =$ `heckeGen ℓ` $\cdot\,$`toPic0Pair` $S(\mathrm{sp}\,x)$, for the module structure on the pair coming from the one fixed in (4).
--
--   (4l) $\mathrm{sp}_N(T\cdot y) = T\cdot \mathrm{sp}_N(y)$ for all $T \in$ `HeckeAlg` and $y \in$ `JZero N`.
--
--   (4m) If $y \in$ `JZero N` is prime-to-$q$ torsion and $\mathrm{sp}_N y = 0$, then $y = 0$.
--
--   (4n) Every prime-to-$q$ torsion class $c \in \mathrm{Pic}^0(F)$ is $\mathrm{sp}_N y$ for some prime-to-$q$ torsion $y \in$ `JZero N`.
--
--   (4o) For every $\sigma \in$ `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` and every prime-to-$q$ torsion $x \in J_0(Nq)$: the element $\sigma\cdot x - x$ lies in $H$, and for that membership $\mathrm{comp}(\sigma\cdot x - x) = 0$ and `toPic0Pair` $S(\mathrm{sp}(\sigma\cdot x - x)) = 0$.
--
--   (4p) For every $m$ coprime to $q$ and every $g' \in$ `GluedPic0` $\kappa\,F\,S$ with $m\,g' = 0$, there is $x \in H$ with $m\,x = 0$, $\mathrm{comp}\,x = 0$ and $\mathrm{sp}\,x = g'$.
--
--   (4q) For every $m$ coprime to $q$ and every $\phi$ in the component group with $m\,\phi = 0$, there is $x \in H$ with $m\,x = 0$ and $\mathrm{comp}\,x = \phi$.
--
--   (4r) For every $\sigma$ in the decomposition subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb Q$ and every $x \in H$ with $\sigma\cdot x \in H$: $\mathrm{comp}\,x = 0$ implies $\mathrm{comp}(\sigma\cdot x) = 0$.
--
--   (4s) For such $\sigma$ and $x$: if moreover `toPic0Pair` $S(\mathrm{sp}\,x) = 0$, then `toPic0Pair` $S(\mathrm{sp}(\sigma\cdot x)) = 0$.
--
--   (4t) For such $\sigma$ and $x$ with $\mathrm{comp}\,x = 0$, and for all $a, b \in$ `JZero N`: if `toPic0Pair` $S(\mathrm{sp}\,x) = (\mathrm{sp}_N a, \mathrm{sp}_N b)$, then `toPic0Pair` $S(\mathrm{sp}(\sigma\cdot x)) = (\mathrm{sp}_N(\sigma\cdot a), \mathrm{sp}_N(\sigma\cdot b))$.
--
--   (4u) There is a `HeckeAlg`-module structure on the component group `componentGroup (widthOfPlaces` $g\,W\,e)$ such that: $\mathrm{comp}(T\cdot x) = T\cdot \mathrm{comp}\,x$ for all $T \in$ `HeckeAlg` and $x \in H$ with $T\cdot x \in H$; and, for every maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$ of `HeckeAlg` whose $\mathfrak m$-torsion `heckeTorsion` in the component group is trivial, every $x$ in the $\mathfrak m$-torsion of $J_0(Nq)$ which is prime-to-$q$ torsion, lies in $H$, and satisfies $\mathrm{comp}\,x = 0$ and `toPic0Pair` $S(\mathrm{sp}\,x) = 0$, belongs to `toricMonodromyPart q (A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ)`, the `HeckeAlg`-span of the elements $\sigma\cdot y - y$ with $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup and $y$ killed by some positive integer coprime to $q$.
--
--   (4v) $e\,w =$ `placeWidthChar q N w` for every $w \in W$, where the right-hand side is `jWidthChar q` of the value of $w$ at `jGeomGen` $\kappa\,N$ divided by `placeRamificationJ N w`.
--
--   (4w) `R.IsModel` — the conjunction of the two divisor laws and the two cusp laws `DivisorLawFst`, `DivisorLawSnd`, `CuspLawInfty`, `CuspLawZero` — and `R.OrderLawFixed`: for every $f$ lying in the integer rings of both $R_1$ and $R_2$ with nonzero residues, every divisor $D$ computing the orders of $f$, and every affine geometric place $v$ of $F$ fixed by the square of `frobOnPlacesGeomLevel`, the value at $v$ of the pushforward of $D$ along `P.reduceFst` equals $\mathrm{ord}_v$ of the first residue of $f$ plus $\mathrm{ord}$ at `frobOnPlacesGeomLevel` $v$ of its second residue.
--
--   (4x) `R.RegularityLaw W` and `R.NodeValueLaw W`. The regularity law has two clauses: for $f$ in both integer rings and $v$ an affine geometric place fixed by the square of `frobOnPlacesGeomLevel`, if $\mathrm{ord}_V f \ge 0$ for every place $V$ at level $Nq$ with `P.reduceFst` $V = v$, then the first residue of $f$ has nonnegative order at $v$ when it is nonzero, and the second residue has nonnegative order at `frobOnPlacesGeomLevel` $v$ when it is nonzero; and for every node pair $s \in S$, if $\mathrm{ord}_V f \ge 0$ for every $V$ with `P.reduceFst` $V = s_1$, then there is $c \in \kappa$ with the first residue of $f$ taking the value $c$ at $s_1$ and the second residue taking the value $c$ at $s_2$. The node value law states that for $f$ in both integer rings with both residues nonzero and for $s \in S$ such that no place $V$ with $\mathrm{ord}_V f \ne 0$ satisfies `P.reduceFst` $V = s_1$ and `P.reduceSnd` $V = s_2$, there is $c \in \kappa$, $c \ne 0$, such that the first residue of $f$ has value $c$ at $s_1$ and the second residue has value $c$ at $s_2$.
--
--   This is the single-package form of the Deligne–Rapoport description of $J_0(Nq)$ at a prime $q\nmid N$: the special fibre is seen through two copies of $X_0(N)$ over the residue field glued along the supersingular places, with a component group attached to the node widths, Hecke and Galois compatibilities for the component map and the glued specialisation, and the toric part cut out by inertia coboundaries. All of these data are produced simultaneously, so that downstream constructions — the Néron object and bridge statement [`ModularCurve.DRModelPackageLevel.exists_jZeroNeronObjectAtP_and_bridge_representsRelSubPic_abqFibre_of_levelModel`](thm.html#ModularCurve.DRModelPackageLevel.exists_jZeroNeronObjectAtP_and_bridge_representsRelSubPic_abqFibre_of_levelModel), and the semistable specialisation package [`ModularCurve.nonempty_jZeroSemistableSpecialization_neronClauses_nodes_heckeTransport_inertiaNodeUnit`](thm.html#ModularCurve.nonempty_jZeroSemistableSpecialization_neronClauses_nodes_heckeTransport_inertiaNodeUnit) used in the level-lowering argument — can work with one coherent choice of witnesses.
